 ## Overview
-Barycentric interpolation on triangles evaluates vertex attributes (normals, colors, UVs) at a ray hit point using barycentric coordinates [S2, unofficial]. It provides a linearly varying field over the 2D simplex defined by the triangle. Unlike [Barycentric Interpolation on Tetrahedra](1843-barycentric-interpolation-on-tetrahedra.md), which operates in 3D volume using four vertices, this method operates on the 2D surface using three vertices. It is distinct from [Clough–Tocher Bézier Patch Interpolation](9134-clough-tocher-b-zier-patch-interpolation.md) by being strictly linear and C0 continuous, which is computationally cheaper but prone to visible shading discontinuities across edges if vertex normals are not shared [S1, unofficial].
+Barycentric interpolation on triangles evaluates vertex attributes (normals, colors, UVs) at a ray hit point using barycentric coordinates [S2, unofficial]. It provides a linearly varying field over the 2D simplex defined by the triangle [G]. Unlike [Barycentric Interpolation on Tetrahedra](1843-barycentric-interpolation-on-tetrahedra.md), which operates in 3D volume using four vertices, this method operates on the 2D surface using three vertices [G]. It is distinct from [Clough–Tocher Bézier Patch Interpolation](9134-clough-tocher-b-zier-patch-interpolation.md) by being strictly linear and C0 continuous, which is computationally cheaper but prone to visible shading discontinuities across edges if vertex normals are not shared [S1, unofficial].
 
